# Who may sign a response with an ssh key (`war sign --ssh-sign`).
#
# Copy to `allowed_signers` and edit — or answer `war init` at a terminal,
# which writes it from the key you pick out of `ssh-add -L`. Like `roles.toml`
# it follows one rule (OW-ADR-0021, Consequences): a tool writes it only from a
# human's answers at a terminal, once; no command edits it afterwards. It is
# the list of keys whose signatures this repository accepts, and a tool that
# wrote it at will could let an agent add its own key.
#
# Format is OpenSSH's ALLOWED SIGNERS (see ssh-keygen(1), "-Y verify"):
#
#   <principal> [namespaces="oh.war/response,oh.war/dsse"] <keytype> <base64> [comment]
#
# `principal` is the `ssh_principal` on the actor's `roles.toml` entry — a
# separate name because OpenSSH permits no spaces here. The namespaces option
# is optional but recommended: it means a signature this key made for any
# other purpose does not verify as a response. If you set it, list BOTH
# namespaces: `oh.war/response` signs the response, `oh.war/dsse` signs the
# attestation `war sign --ssh-sign` writes beside the record (OW-ADR-0015).
# With only the first, the act is recorded and its attestation is refused
# with `attest.not-emitted` — the act stands; the witness is missing.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE THING `war` CANNOT CHECK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Load the key into your agent with confirmation ON:
#
#   ssh-add -c ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
#
# `-c` makes the agent ask YOU, through a dialog, every time the key is used.
# That dialog is the human act; no shell — yours, or an agent's running inside
# your session — can answer it. Without `-c`, any process that can reach the
# agent socket can sign as you, and an AI agent's shell can reach it. `war`
# has no way to tell whether the key was loaded with `-c`. Get this right.
#
# A hardware key (sk-ssh-ed25519@openssh.com) with touch required is the same
# guarantee, held in silicon.
#
# TEST YOUR DENY BEFORE YOU TRUST YOUR ALLOW. The agent raises the dialog
# through $SSH_ASKPASS with SSH_ASKPASS_PROMPT=confirm and treats exit 0 as
# "allow". A custom askpass that only knows passphrase mode, or that parses
# pinentry's replies carelessly, can allow on Deny — found here the first time
# it was used: pinentry answers OK to every SET command before the CONFIRM
# reply, so "any OK" allowed everything. Run `war sign <x> --ssh-sign` once,
# click Deny, and confirm `sign.ssh-refused` with nothing written. Then trust it.
# One more trap for pinentry-based askpasses: the agent's confirm prompt
# contains a literal NEWLINE ("Allow use of key …?\nKey fingerprint …").
# Passed raw, it splits your own SETDESC into a second, unknown command that
# pinentry answers with ERR — and a correct any-ERR-denies rule then denies
# every Allow. Percent-encode it (%0A) before sending.
# Note also that the agent's environment, not your shell's, must carry
# SSH_ASKPASS and DISPLAY — a systemd user service needs a drop-in for that.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

brian namespaces="oh.war/response,oh.war/dsse" ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAA...replace-with-your-key... brian@host
